        ("Elements of Government & Political Theory", "Rule of Law Safeguards", "An essential constitutional safeguard necessary for upholding the Rule of Law in any modern democracy is:", {"A": "Security of tenure and financial independence for judicial officers", "B": "A state-controlled press that praises government policies", "C": "Suspension of constitutional rights during election campaigns", "D": "Allowing the military to arbitrate parliamentary debates"}, "A", "An independent judiciary with secured tenure and consolidated salary funds is vital to check executive arbitrariness.", "How can judges rule impartially without fear of intimidation from the executive arm?"),
        ("Elements of Government & Political Theory", "Bicameralism", "Which of the following is a primary rationale for adopting a bicameral legislature in a federal country like Nigeria?", {"A": "To ensure equal representation of states in the Senate and prevent the domination of small ethnic groups", "B": "To speed up the lawmaking process by avoiding debate", "C": "To concentrate legislative power into the hands of traditional rulers", "D": "To reduce the financial cost of national governance"}, "A", "Bicameralism balances population-based representation (House of Reps) with equality of states (Senate).", "Why do federal nations have both a Senate with equal seats per state and a House of Representatives?"),
        ("Colonial Administration in Nigeria & West Africa", "Warrant Chiefs System", "The British colonial government introduced 'Warrant Chiefs' in Eastern Nigeria because:", {"A": "The decentralized, acephalous political structure of traditional Igbo society lacked hereditary executive monarchs", "B": "The traditional monarchs were all executed in the 1851 bombardment of Lagos", "C": "The Igbo people requested British judicial magistrates", "D": "The region had already been converted into a crown colony in 1861"}, "A", "Because traditional Igbo communities were governed by democratic village assemblies, the British arbitrarily created Warrant Chiefs to collect taxes.", "Why did the British create Warrant Chiefs in the East but used existing Emirs in the North?"),
        ("Colonial Administration in Nigeria & West Africa", "Nationalist Movements", "The first organized nationwide political movement established in 1936 to campaign against British colonial restrictions in Nigeria was:", {"A": "The Nigerian Youth Movement (NYM)", "B": "The Nigerian National Democratic Party (NNDP)", "C": "The Action Group (AG)", "D": "The Northern People's Congress (NPC)"}, "A", "The NYM was the first genuine pan-Nigerian nationalist body uniting leaders across regions (Ikoli, Azikiwe, Awolowo).", "Which youth movement formed in 1936 challenged Herbert Macaulay's Lagos-only NNDP?"),
        ("Nigerian Constitutional Development", "Richards Constitution (1946)", "The Richards Constitution of 1946 was severely criticized by Nigerian nationalists primarily because:", {"A": "It was drafted and enacted without any prior consultation with Nigerian leaders or people", "B": "It granted immediate self-government to Northern Nigeria", "C": "It abolished the traditional chieftaincy titles", "D": "It removed the regional houses of assembly"}, "A", "Arthur Richards authored the constitution in secret without public input, sparking furious nationalist opposition.", "Why was Sir Arthur Richards' 1946 constitution condemned as an arbitrary colonial imposition?"),
        ("Nigerian Constitutional Development", "Lyttelton Constitution (1954)", "Under the Lyttelton Constitution of 1954, legislative subjects on which BOTH the federal and regional legislatures could make laws were placed on the:", {"A": "Concurrent Legislative List", "B": "Exclusive Legislative List", "C": "Residual Legislative List", "D": "Federal Prerogative List"}, "A", "Exclusive list was federal only; Concurrent list was shared (with federal supremacy in case of conflict); Residual was regional.", "What is the name of the legislative list shared jointly by federal and regional parliaments?"),
        ("Federalism, Military Rule & Foreign Policy", "Revenue Allocation Derivation", "The derivation principle in Nigerian fiscal federalism stipulates that:", {"A": "A percentage of revenue should be returned directly to the state where the natural resource was extracted", "B": "All revenues must be shared equally among the 36 states", "C": "Revenue must be disbursed strictly according to landmass", "D": "Only federal ministries are entitled to petroleum royalties"}, "A", "Derivation compensates oil-producing communities for resource exploitation and environmental degradation.", "What principle allocates a share of national resource wealth back to the host state of extraction?"),
        ("Federalism, Military Rule & Foreign Policy", "1966 Coup d'État", "The first military coup d'état in Nigeria took place on:", {"A": "January 15, 1966", "B": "July 29, 1966", "C": "October 1, 1960", "D": "December 31, 1983"}, "A", "Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu led the January 15, 1966 coup which toppled the First Republic.", "On what date in January 1966 was Nigeria's First Republic overthrown by young army majors?"),
        ("Federalism, Military Rule & Foreign Policy", "African Union & Decolonization", "Nigeria earned the prestigious status of an honorary 'Frontline State' in Southern Africa because of:", {"A": "Its massive diplomatic, financial, and material support for the anti-apartheid liberation movements in South Africa, Namibia, and Zimbabwe", "B": "Its geographic location on the border of South Africa", "C": "Its colonial ties with Mozambique", "D": "Its membership in the Warsaw Pact"}, "A", "Despite being in West Africa, Nigeria's decisive anti-apartheid leadership earned it honorary Frontline State recognition.", "Why was Nigeria called a Frontline State during the struggle to defeat apartheid in South Africa?"),
        ("Elements of Government & Political Theory", "Civil Service Neutrality", "The constitutional principle of civil service anonymity requires that:", {"A": "Civil servants remain behind the scenes, leaving political responsibility and public defense of policies to the Minister", "B": "Civil servants must never disclose their names on official letters", "C": "Civil servants cannot be dismissed under any circumstances", "D": "Ministers cannot sign executive orders"}, "A", "Ministers shoulder political praise or blame in parliament, while permanent civil servants remain anonymous and politically neutral.", "Why do government ministers defend policies in the media instead of the permanent secretaries?"),
